  "prediction": "The report discusses the mail and wire fraud statutes, which are federal laws that prohibit schemes to defraud or obtain money or property by using the mail or wire communications. These statutes have been in place since the late 1800s and have been used to prosecute a wide range of fraudulent activities. The mail fraud statute predates the wire fraud statute and was originally intended to prevent schemes targeting rural areas where mail was the primary means of communication. The wire fraud statute was added later to address schemes using telephone communications. Both statutes make it illegal to use mail or wire communications to execute a scheme to defraud or obtain money or property by false pretenses. The statutes are similar, with the main difference being the use of mail or wire communications as the means to further the fraud. Both statutes carry penalties of up to 20 years in prison and fines. The report also mentions that attempts or conspiracies to commit mail or wire fraud are also illegal and carry the same penalties as the underlying offense. The mail and wire fraud statutes have been used to prosecute various types of fraud, including securities fraud, healthcare fraud, and mortgage fraud. The report concludes by noting that due to the broad scope of these statutes, it is important for individuals and businesses to be aware of the potential for mail and wire fraud and take steps to protect themselves.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the mail and wire fraud statutes, which are federal laws that prohibit schemes to defraud or obtain money or property by using the mail or wire communications. These statutes have been in place since the late 1800s and have been used to prosecute a wide range of fraudulent activities. The mail fraud statute predates the wire fraud statute and was originally intended to prevent schemes targeting rural areas where mail was the primary means of communication. The wire fraud statute was added later to address schemes using telephone communications. Both statutes make it illegal to use mail or wire communications to execute a scheme to defraud or obtain money or property by false pretenses. The statutes are similar, with the main difference being the use of mail or wire communications as the means to further the fraud. Both statutes carry penalties of up to 20 years in prison and fines. The report also mentions that attempts or conspiracies to commit mail or wire fraud are also illegal and carry the same penalties as the underlying offense. The mail and wire fraud statutes have been used to prosecute various types of fraud, including securities fraud, healthcare fraud, and mortgage fraud. The report concludes by noting that due to the broad scope of these statutes, it is important for individuals and businesses to be aware of the potential for mail and wire fraud and take steps to protect themselves.",
